  "preamble": "Fortition of the labio-velar glide [w] to the voiced bilabial stop [b]. In the transition from Middle Persian to New Persian, word-initial w- became b- (alongside a gw- > g- outcome in other words): Middle Persian wād 'wind' and wahār 'spring' became bād and bahār. Independently, Proto-Dravidian initial *w- became b- in Kannada and Tulu, while Tamil, Malayalam and Telugu keep v-. The North Dravidian languages Brahui, Kurukh and Malto also have b- for Proto-Dravidian *w- (*wāy 'mouth' > Brahui bā, Kurukh baī). In the Zan branch of Kartvelian, Proto-Georgian-Zan *w became b in words such as *wenaq- 'vine' > Laz, Mingrelian binexi and *ḳraw- 'lamb' > Mingrelian ḳiribi, where Old Georgian keeps ვ (venaqi, ḳravi).",
